  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nSatellite and radar imagery show quickly decrease coverage of\nlight and widely scattered rain showers over north-cental\nWisconsin this afternoon. For the rest of the day, northerly \nwinds will quickly decrease from west to east as high pressure \nmoves overhead tonight under a passing mid-level ridge.\n\nExpect a wind shift to the southwest overnight, with winds \nactually increasing in speed as a southwesterly low-level jet \nbuilds over northern Minnesota early Saturday morning. This \nsetup creates the lowest temperatures tonight in north-central \nMinnesota shortly after midnight, then stabilizing or actually \nincreasing towards the dawn hours Saturday morning. Meanwhile, \ncalm winds under strong radiational cooling for northwest \nWisconsin will allow temperatures to continue to drop out into \nthe mid to upper 20s tonight. Expect a hard freeze tonight, but \nthe Duluth office has ended the frost and freeze headlines for \nthe season given earlier widespread freeze conditions, so no \nheadlines are in effect for this hard freeze.\n\nSaturday will feature breezy southwest winds as drier air mixes down\nto the surface. The forecast continues to resemble about a 20 to\n30th percentile of guidance for relative humidity tomorrow, which\nputs minimum relative humidity values of 30 to 38 percent in the\nforecast. These values are just above near-critical conditions\nand have actually trended upwards in recent forecasts, so no \nSpecial Weather Statements are in effect covering Saturday. \nHowever, folks should still be aware of an elevated fire weather\nrisk Saturday mid-day to early evening due to the combination \nof this semi-dry air and breezy winds.\n\nThe pattern shifts to feature no precipitation and warming Sunday\nonward. Continued breezy southwest winds Sunday morning areawide\nwill decrease in speed from northwest to southeast during the \nafternoon. High temperatures will continue their upward climb, \nand we will be back into the mid 60s for the next work week with\npredominantly dry weather dominating the Northland. The\nexception will be a couple of days of lake breezes keeping\nshoreline areas right at normal for mid-October.\n\nHeading deeper into the next work week, the predominantly quiet\nand unseasonably mild weather remains locked in as an upper- \nlevel ridge anchors itself over the central portion of the \nContinent and two coastal low pressure systems remain over the \nMid-Atlantic and California/Baja region. There are very low- \nend, 10 to 20 percent, chances of light rain late next work week\nas a ripple moving through the broad and warm upper- level \nridge may occur. However, there is very low confidence on this \nscenario at this time, and dry conditions remain heavily \nfavored.",
